<div class="space-y-12">
    <div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Replication &amp; Cluster</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-400">Master/replica replication with PSYNC, and the Redis Cluster protocol: 16384 slots, redirections, resharding and automatic failover</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Architecture -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-6">Three levels of distribution</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-4">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Shards in a node</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">Each node splits its keys over <code class="text-slate-300">--shards</code> shards (one per core by default), each behind its own lock. Automatic; clients never see it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-purple-400 mb-2">Replication</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">A replica follows a master's write stream: read scaling, a hot standby, and the replicas cluster failover promotes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-cyan-400 mb-2">Redis Cluster</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">Several masters own the 16384 slots; cluster-aware clients route each key to its owner and follow redirections.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Internal shards -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Shards within a node</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4">A key goes to the shard of its Redis Cluster slot (CRC16 of the key, or of its <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">{hash tag}</code>) modulo the shard count. Commands that span shards &mdash; <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">RENAME</code>, <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">MSET</code>, <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">SUNIONSTORE</code>, transactions, scripts &mdash; lock every shard involved in a fixed order, so they stay atomic. Keys are re-hashed when loaded, so the shard count can change between restarts.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead><tr class="border-b border-white/10"><th class="py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Value</th><th class="py-3 text-slate-400 font-medium">Behavior</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody class="text-slate-300">
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">--shards 0</td><td class="py-3">One shard per CPU core (default)</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">--shards N</td><td class="py-3">N shards</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">--solo</td><td class="py-3">One unlocked shard on one worker thread, shaped like Redis' event loop</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Replication -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Replication</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4">A replica connects to its master, does a full resynchronization from a snapshot, then applies the master's write stream. After a disconnect it resumes from the master's backlog (<code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--repl-backlog-size</code>, 1mb) with <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">PSYNC</code> when it can, and resynchronizes in full otherwise. Writes that span several shards reach replicas as one <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">MULTI</code>/<code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">EXEC</code> block, so a replica never shows half of one.</p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 overflow-hidden mb-4">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-mono">Terminal</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 font-mono text-sm space-y-1">
                <div class="text-slate-500"># Master</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --port 6379 --dir /data/master --requirepass-file /etc/solikv/password</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># Replica: at startup ("host port" or host:port) ...</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --port 6380 --dir /data/replica --replicaof "10.0.0.1 6379" --masterauth "$MASTER_PASSWORD"</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># ... or at runtime</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:6380&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">REPLICAOF 10.0.0.1 6379</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">OK</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:6380&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">ROLE</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">1) "slave"</div>
                <div class="text-white">2) "10.0.0.1"</div>
                <div class="text-white">3) (integer) 6379</div>
                <div class="text-white">4) "connected"</div>
                <div class="text-white">5) (integer) 167</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># On the master: wait until 1 replica acknowledged this client's writes</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:6379&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">SET k v</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">OK</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:6379&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">WAIT 1 1000</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">(integer) 1</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># Promote the replica by hand</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:6380&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">REPLICAOF NO ONE</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">OK</div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">Replica behaviour</h3>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-400">
                    <li>&bull; Read-only by default (<code class="text-slate-300">--replica-read-only</code>): writes get <code class="text-slate-300">READONLY You can't write against a read only replica.</code></li>
                    <li>&bull; Does not expire keys on its own clock: the master sends the <code class="text-slate-300">DEL</code>; keys past their deadline already read as missing.</li>
                    <li>&bull; Writes its own append-only log from what it applies; replicas of replicas get the master's stream unchanged.</li>
                    <li>&bull; A replica can also follow a Redis master and load its snapshot.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">Master side</h3>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-400">
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">INFO replication</code> lists each replica's offset and lag; <code class="text-slate-300">ROLE</code> too.</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">--min-replicas-to-write N</code> with <code class="text-slate-300">--min-replicas-max-lag</code> refuses writes (<code class="text-slate-300">NOREPLICAS</code>) without enough fresh replicas.</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">--tls-replication yes</code> encrypts the link; <code class="text-slate-300">--masteruser</code> authenticates as an ACL user.</li>
                    <li>&bull; Until a replica first connects, replication costs one atomic load per write.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">There is no Sentinel: a standalone master/replica pair is failed over by hand. Automatic failover exists in cluster mode.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Cluster -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Redis Cluster</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4">Start every node with <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--cluster-enabled</code> and the same bus secret (at least 32 bytes, in a 0600 file). The cluster bus listens on the data port + 10000 (<code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--cluster-port</code>). Then form the cluster with the standard <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">redis-cli --cluster</code> tool.</p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 overflow-hidden mb-4">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-mono">Terminal &mdash; 3 masters, 3 replicas</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 font-mono text-sm space-y-1">
                <div class="text-slate-500"># A shared bus key</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 &gt; bus.secret &amp;&amp; chmod 600 bus.secret</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># Six nodes</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">for p in 7000 7001 7002 7003 7004 7005; do</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400 ml-4">solikv --port $p --bind 127.0.0.1 --rest-port 0 --dir data/$p \</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400 ml-8">--cluster-enabled --cluster-bus-secret-file bus.secret &amp;</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">done</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># Assign slots and replicas</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli --cluster create 127.0.0.1:7000 127.0.0.1:7001 127.0.0.1:7002 \</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400 ml-4">127.0.0.1:7003 127.0.0.1:7004 127.0.0.1:7005 --cluster-replicas 1</div>
                <div class="text-white">[OK] All nodes agree about slots configuration.</div>
                <div class="text-white">[OK] All 16384 slots covered.</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># Use it with a cluster-aware client (-c follows MOVED/ASK)</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli -c -p 7000 SET user:42 alice</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 mt-8">Routing</h3>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm mb-4">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-400">
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">MOVED slot host:port</code> &mdash; the key's slot belongs to another node.</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">ASK slot host:port</code> &mdash; the slot is being migrated and the key already moved; the client retries there after <code class="text-slate-300">ASKING</code>.</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">CROSSSLOT</code> &mdash; a multi-key command whose keys hash to different slots; use <code class="text-slate-300">{hash tags}</code>.</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">TRYAGAIN</code>, <code class="text-slate-300">CLUSTERDOWN</code> &mdash; during migration, or while a slot has no working owner (<code class="text-slate-300">--cluster-require-full-coverage</code>).</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">READONLY</code> lets a client read from replicas.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 p-4 font-mono text-sm space-y-1">
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:7000&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">CLUSTER KEYSLOT mykey</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">(integer) 14687</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:7000&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">GET mykey</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">(error) MOVED 14687 127.0.0.1:7002</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:7000&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">MSET a 1 zz 2</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">(error) CROSSSLOT Keys in request don't hash to the same slot</div>
                <div><span class="text-slate-500">127.0.0.1:7000&gt;</span> <span class="text-emerald-400">MSET {u}a 1 {u}b 2</span></div>
                <div class="text-white">(error) MOVED 11826 127.0.0.1:7002</div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-8"><code class="text-slate-300">CLUSTER SLOTS</code>, <code class="text-slate-300">CLUSTER SHARDS</code> and <code class="text-slate-300">CLUSTER NODES</code> reply in Redis 7's format, so the cluster modes of redis-py, ioredis, go-redis, Jedis/Lettuce and redis-rs work unchanged. <code class="text-slate-300">PUBLISH</code> reaches subscribers on every node; <code class="text-slate-300">SPUBLISH</code> stays on the slot's owner.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Resharding</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4">Moving slots follows the Redis procedure (<code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">CLUSTER SETSLOT … IMPORTING/MIGRATING</code>, <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">CLUSTER GETKEYSINSLOT</code>, <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">MIGRATE</code>, <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">SETSLOT … NODE</code>), so the standard tool does it while clients keep writing:</p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 p-4 font-mono text-sm space-y-1 mb-8">
            <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli --cluster reshard 127.0.0.1:7000 --cluster-from &lt;source-id&gt; --cluster-to &lt;target-id&gt; --cluster-slots 500</div>
            <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli --cluster check 127.0.0.1:7000</div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Failure detection and failover</h3>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm mb-4">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h4 class="font-semibold mb-2">Automatic</h4>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-400">
                    <li>&bull; A node unreachable for <code class="text-slate-300">--cluster-node-timeout</code> (15 s) is suspected (PFAIL), and marked FAIL once a majority of slot-owning masters report it.</li>
                    <li>&bull; Its replicas hold an election; masters grant one vote per epoch, and the winner takes the slots with a new config epoch.</li>
                    <li>&bull; A master left without working replicas can receive one from a master that has spare (<code class="text-slate-300">--cluster-allow-replica-migration</code>, <code class="text-slate-300">--cluster-migration-barrier</code>).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h4 class="font-semibold mb-2">Manual</h4>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-400">
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">CLUSTER FAILOVER</code> on a replica: coordinated with its master &mdash; the master stops accepting writes, the replica catches up with its replication offset, then takes over.</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">CLUSTER FAILOVER FORCE</code>: without the master's cooperation.</li>
                    <li>&bull; <code class="text-slate-300">CLUSTER FAILOVER TAKEOVER</code>: without an election.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-8">Each node saves its view of the cluster (node ids, epochs, slots) to <code class="text-slate-300">&lt;dir&gt;/cluster.json</code> on every change and reloads it at startup.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Bus security</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4">Every frame between nodes is signed with HMAC-SHA-256 under the bus secret, bound to its receiving node and protected against replay. <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--tls-cluster yes</code> also encrypts the bus with mutual TLS (certificates from <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--tls-ca-cert</code>). The bus secret is separate from client passwords. See <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">SECURITY.md</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- CLUSTER commands -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">CLUSTER commands</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead><tr class="border-b border-white/10"><th class="py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Command</th><th class="py-3 text-slate-400 font-medium">Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody class="text-slate-300">
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">INFO / MYID / MYSHARDID</td><td class="py-3">State, slot counts, epochs; this node's ids</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">SLOTS / SHARDS / NODES / LINKS</td><td class="py-3">Topology in Redis 7's formats</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">KEYSLOT / COUNTKEYSINSLOT / GETKEYSINSLOT</td><td class="py-3">Slot of a key; keys stored in a slot</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">MEET / FORGET / RESET</td><td class="py-3">Membership</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">ADDSLOTS / ADDSLOTSRANGE / DELSLOTS / DELSLOTSRANGE / FLUSHSLOTS</td><td class="py-3">Slot assignment</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">SETSLOT slot IMPORTING|MIGRATING|STABLE|NODE</td><td class="py-3">Slot migration</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">REPLICATE / REPLICAS / SLAVES</td><td class="py-3">Make this node a replica; list a master's replicas</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">FAILOVER [FORCE|TAKEOVER]</td><td class="py-3">Manual failover</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">SAVECONFIG / BUMPEPOCH / SET-CONFIG-EPOCH / COUNT-FAILURE-REPORTS</td><td class="py-3">Maintenance</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Dump / restore -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Exporting and importing a cluster</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4">The <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">solikv</code> binary can also dump every key of a running cluster to a file and load it into another one. Keys are listed with <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">SCAN</code> and saved as their <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">DUMP</code> payload with their TTL, then restored with <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">RESTORE … REPLACE</code>; a dump that misses a node, or a restore with any error, fails. Given the nodes' <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--tls-cert</code>/<code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--tls-key</code>/<code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--tls-ca-cert</code>, it connects over TLS. Files written by 0.4.x still restore.</p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 p-4 font-mono text-sm space-y-1">
            <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --cluster-connect 127.0.0.1:7000 --cluster-dump backup.jsonl --cluster-password-file pw</div>
            <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --cluster-connect 127.0.0.1:8000 --cluster-restore backup.jsonl --cluster-password-file pw</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Upgrading -->
    <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Upgrading a 0.4.x cluster</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">0.4.x computed key slots with a wrong CRC16 table, so its nodes hold keys under slots that do not match Redis (and 0.5). Its bus protocol also differs, so 0.4.x and 0.5 nodes cannot form one cluster. 0.4.x's own <code class="text-slate-300">--cluster-dump</code> lost data, so do not migrate with it. Instead, for each old node, start a <em>copy</em> of its data directory with 0.5 as a standalone server (its files are migrated on load), then copy its keys into a new 0.5 cluster with <code class="text-slate-300">redis-cli --cluster import 127.0.0.1:7000 --cluster-from 127.0.0.1:7400 --cluster-copy</code>. Use <code class="text-slate-300">--cluster-bus-secret-file</code> rather than <code class="text-slate-300">--cluster-password</code> as the bus key.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Testing -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Testing</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400">The integration tests in <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">crates/solikv-server/tests/</code> start real server processes: <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">cluster_6.rs</code> (a three-master cluster driven by redis-rs' cluster client, resharding with <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">MIGRATE</code>, automatic and manual failover), <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">replication_6.rs</code> (full and partial resync, <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">WAIT</code>, expiry on replicas), <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">cluster_7b.rs</code> and <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">tls_7b.rs</code> (replica migration, TLS bus and replication).</p>
    </div>
</div>
